import subprocess,os
CSS="""<style>@page{size:A4;margin:18mm 16mm}body{font-family:Arial,sans-serif;color:#222;font-size:10.5pt;line-height:1.45}
h1{font-size:30pt;margin:0;font-style:italic;color:#1a1a1a}.sub{color:#e0601a;font-size:13pt;margin-bottom:16px}
h2{color:#c8361d;border-bottom:2px solid #f08a24;padding-bottom:3px;margin-top:20px}section{break-inside:avoid}
img{width:100%;border:1px solid #444;margin:6px 0}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%}td,th{border:1px solid #bbb;padding:4px 6px;vertical-align:top;text-align:left}th{background:#f6e3c9}
.stripe{height:5px;background:linear-gradient(90deg,#d62e1f 25%,#f57a1f 25% 50%,#fad542 50% 75%,#ede6cc 75%);margin:8px 0 18px}.warn{background:#fff3e0;border-left:4px solid #f08a24;padding:8px 10px}
</style>"""
def page(title,body): return "<html><head><meta charset=utf-8>"+CSS+"</head><body>"+body+"</body></html>"
man="""<h1>DIODE 808</h1><div class=sub>Rhythm Composer for Max for Live — User Manual · version 1.0.0</div><div class=stripe></div>
<img src="bd.png">
<section><h2>Contents</h2><ol><li>Overview</li><li>System Requirements</li><li>Installation</li><li>The Panel</li><li>Sound Controls</li><li>The Hidden Controls</li><li>Programming Patterns</li><li>Flam</li><li>Shared Circuits: Toms and Congas, Rim Shot and Claves</li><li>Patterns, Chains, Mute and Solo</li><li>MIDI</li><li>Credits and Licence</li></ol></section>
<section><h2>1. Overview</h2><p>DIODE 808 is a TR-808 for Ableton Live. It joins an x0x step sequencer with the <b>Diode 808 circuit engine</b>: every voice of the 808 is simulated from the original schematics at transistor level, so the drums react to the knobs, the accent and to each other just like the hardware.</p><p>DIODE 808 runs inside Live, works with Push 3, and stores its patterns with your Live Set.</p></section>
<section><h2>2. System Requirements</h2><table><tr><th>Item</th><th>Requirement</th></tr><tr><td>Ableton Live</td><td>Live 12 Suite (or Live 12 Standard with Max for Live)</td></tr><tr><td>macOS</td><td>macOS 12 Monterey or later, Apple Silicon or Intel</td></tr><tr><td>Windows</td><td>Windows 10 or 11, 64-bit</td></tr><tr><td>CPU</td><td>A circuit simulation is heavier than a sample player: a recent multi-core CPU (Apple M1 or better) is recommended. DIODE 808 spreads its voices over several cores.</td></tr><tr><td>Push</td><td>Compatible with Push 3 controlling Live</td></tr></table></section>
<section><h2>3. Installation</h2><p>Drop <b>DIODE 808.amxd</b> onto a MIDI track in Live, or save it to your User Library (Max for Live → Max Instrument) to find it in the browser. The device is self-contained: nothing else needs installing.</p></section>
<section><h2>4. The Panel</h2><p><b>Top strip:</b> mute (M) and solo (S) buttons for the 15 tracks; the INSTRUMENT SELECT knob with its labels; the sound controls of the selected track; LAST STEP, CHANNEL and NOTE displays; the DIODE 808 logo (click it to open the store).</p><p><b>Bottom section:</b> WRITE, CLEAR, COPY, PASTE; the 16 step keys with their LEDs in four beat groups; the 16 pattern buttons.</p>
<p>Click a track name above the mute buttons, a label beside the knob, or drag the knob to select a track. Clicking the label of a pair (LT·LC, MT·MC, HT·HC, RS·CL) again switches between its two sounds.</p></section>
<section><h2>5. Sound Controls</h2><p>The knob area shows the controls of the selected track — the controls of the 808's front panel. Drag up or down to turn; Shift-drag for fine moves; double-click to go back to the factory position (the red dot). Values read 0–10 like the hardware. Every control is a Live parameter: automate it or map it to Push.</p>
<table><tr><th>Track</th><th>Controls</th></tr><tr><td>AC (Accent)</td><td>ACCENT level · FLAM time · VOLUME (master) · NOISE (hidden control)</td></tr><tr><td>BD Bass Drum</td><td>LEVEL · TONE · DECAY · TUNE (hidden control)</td></tr><tr><td>SD Snare Drum</td><td>LEVEL · TONE · SNAPPY · REV (hidden control)</td></tr><tr><td>LT/LC, MT/MC, HT/HC</td><td>LEVEL · TUNING (shared by the tom and the conga)</td></tr><tr><td>RS/CL Rim Shot / Claves</td><td>LEVEL</td></tr><tr><td>CP Hand Clap</td><td>LEVEL · OFFSET (hidden) · REV (hidden)</td></tr><tr><td>CB Cowbell</td><td>LEVEL</td></tr><tr><td>CY Cymbal</td><td>LEVEL · TONE · DECAY</td></tr><tr><td>OH Open Hat</td><td>LEVEL · DECAY</td></tr><tr><td>CH Closed Hat</td><td>LEVEL</td></tr></table><img src="sd.png"></section>
<section><h2>6. The Hidden Controls</h2><p>Inside a real 808 are trimmers and parts that set how each unit sounds. DIODE 808 gives you five of them, all live while the pattern plays:</p><table>
<tr><td><b>Kick Tune</b> (BD, ±12 semitones)</td><td>Changes the two capacitors (C41, C42) of the bass drum's resonant filter, retuning the kick the way a technician would. It glides smoothly while you turn it.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Snare Revision</b> (SD, EARLY / LATE)</td><td>Roland changed parts of the snare circuit during production. LATE (factory) uses the later values (C58 0.056 µF, C61 0.015 µF, R191 10 kΩ): a fuller, longer snare. EARLY uses the schematic's first values (0.027 µF, 0.0068 µF, 8.2 kΩ): shorter, thinner and brighter. Click the switch to flip it.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Clap Revision</b> (CP, EARLY / LATE)</td><td>Later units have a resistor (R200, 10 kΩ) on the clap's envelope transistor; early units leave it out. LATE (factory) gives the familiar clap; EARLY changes its envelope and the snap of the claps.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Clap Offset</b> (CP, 0–100 %, factory 37 %)</td><td>The bias of the clap's sawtooth (the repeated 'claps'): it shifts how the bursts sit against the reverb tail.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Noise</b> (AC, 20–350 mV, factory 190 mV)</td><td>The level of the shared white-noise generator that feeds the snare, the clap and the maracas circuit: more noise gives a hissier snare and a denser clap.</td></tr></table></section>
<section><h2>7. Programming Patterns</h2><p>Select a track, then click step keys to turn steps on or off (drag across keys to paint). The LED above a key shows the step. Select AC to place accents: accented steps are marked with a cream number on every track. Accented steps play louder and punchier — the 808's accent changes the circuits, not just the volume; the ACCENT knob sets how much.</p><p>LAST STEP (drag) sets the pattern length, 1–16 steps. CLEAR empties the pattern (Shift-click: only the selected track). COPY and PASTE copy a whole pattern. With WRITE on and Live playing, MIDI notes you play are recorded into the playing pattern (velocity 120 or more records an accent).</p></section>
<section><h2>8. Flam</h2><p><b>Alt/Option-click</b> a step key to make that step a flam: its LED turns <b style="color:#2a7bff">blue</b>. Alt-click it again to make it a normal step. A flam plays a quiet grace note (unaccented) and then the main hit a moment later. The FLAM knob (on the AC track) sets that time, 5 to 40 ms (factory 19 ms). The main hit keeps the step's accent.</p><img src="bd.png"><p>Above: the bass drum with a flam on step 15.</p></section>
<section><h2>9. Shared Circuits</h2><p>As on the 808, the low, mid and high toms share their circuits with the low, mid and high congas, and the rim shot shares with the claves. DIODE 808 switches the circuit on every hit: you can program toms and congas freely in the same pattern. If both sounds of a pair fall on the same step, the alternate sound (the conga or the claves) plays, because one circuit can make only one sound at a time. The tom and conga of a pair share the LEVEL and TUNING knobs.</p></section>
<section><h2>10. Patterns, Chains, Mute and Solo</h2><p>Click a pattern button to select it; while Live plays, the new pattern starts when the current one ends (it blinks while waiting). Shift-click pattern buttons to build a chain of up to 32 patterns played in order; click a pattern without Shift to clear the chain. M mutes a track, S solos it (Cmd/Ctrl-click S: solo only that track). The playhead follows Live's transport, sample-accurate.</p></section>
<section><h2>11. MIDI</h2><table><tr><th>Sound</th><th>Note</th><th>Sound</th><th>Note</th></tr><tr><td>BD</td><td>C1 36</td><td>CB</td><td>G#2 56</td></tr><tr><td>SD</td><td>D1 38</td><td>CY</td><td>C#2 49</td></tr><tr><td>LT</td><td>G1 43</td><td>CH</td><td>F#1 42</td></tr><tr><td>MT</td><td>B1 47</td><td>OH</td><td>A#1 46</td></tr><tr><td>HT</td><td>D2 50</td><td>LC / MC / HC</td><td>E3 64 / D#3 63 / D3 62</td></tr><tr><td>RS</td><td>C#1 37</td><td>CL</td><td>D#4 75</td></tr><tr><td>CP</td><td>D#1 39</td><td colspan=2>Velocity 100 or more plays accented.</td></tr></table>
<p>On the edit channel (CHANNEL display, default 16): notes 36–51 toggle steps 1–16, notes 52–67 select tracks, notes 72–87 select patterns 1–16.</p></section>
<section><h2>12. Credits and Licence</h2><p>DIODE 808 by Transistor Labs (Ticco Ross). Circuit engine: the tr808 voice board by Lemus, GNU AGPLv3. TR-808 is a trademark of Roland Corporation, which is not associated with this product.</p><p>Contact: ticcoross@gmail.com · Store: https://ticcoross.gumroad.com/</p></section>"""
hist="""<h1>DIODE 808</h1><div class=sub>Max for Live — Update History</div><div class=stripe></div>
<section><h2>1.0.0</h2><ul><li>First release.</li><li>The Diode 808 circuit engine inside the x0x sequencer panel.</li><li>Sound controls of the 808 front panel; master volume.</li><li>Hidden controls: Kick Tune, Snare Revision, Clap Revision, Clap Offset, Noise — all live.</li><li>Flam: Alt/Option-click steps, blue LED, FLAM time 5–40 ms.</li><li>Toms/congas and rim shot/claves switch circuits per hit.</li><li>16 patterns, chains, mute/solo, MIDI, WRITE.</li></ul></section>"""
for name,b in [("DIODE 808 - User Manual",man),("DIODE 808 - Update History",hist)]:
    open("_t.html","w").write(page(name,b))
    subprocess.run(["google-chrome","--headless","--no-sandbox","--disable-gpu","--no-pdf-header-footer","--print-to-pdf="+name+".pdf","_t.html"],check=True,capture_output=True)
os.remove("_t.html")
